From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Mon, 27 Jun 1994 18:15:49 -0700
Subject: [#94-6-173] Evolution of Time Preference

How much should creatures evolved by expect natural selection value
the future?  This question is basic to understanding our own values
about the future, to understanding current social processes, and to
predicting the values of our descendants.

This question is dealt with in:

 Alan Rogers, "The Evolution of Time Preference by Natural Selection",
   American Economic Review, June 1994, V84 N3, pp.460-481.

Here are a few of their results:

1) An asexually-reproducing creature should discount the future at
   exactly the rate of population growth.  If population is growing at
   2% per year, and an asexual creature can get more than that for
   sure in a long-term investment to be paid to its descendants, and if
   certain amount of wealth is equally useful in making babies at all
   times, then its genes will do better if it invests. 

2) A sexually-reproducing creature discounts the future more heavily.
   Since children only share half the genes of a parent, if the
   population growth rate is zero, then a parent requires that their 
   wealth double in one generation in order to make it worth forgoing
   consumption now to allow more consumption for a child.

3) If current human values evolved when population growth rates were 
   very small, then for a typical 29 year generation time, this implies a
   2.4% real interest rate, in rough agreement with observation.

4) Since the evolutionary point of consuming is to make or help babies,
   the rate at which a person will trade off their own consumption at 
   one time verses another with depend on where the two times fall in 
   a person's fertility life cycle.  Young children badly want to invest
   in the future, 15-25 year olds discount the future heavily, and then
   30-50 year olds invest in their future.  This is in rough agreement 
   with observed behavior.

I think these results have some implications for various extropian questions.

A) If you think of yourself as an asexual reproducer, planning to live a
   very long time yourself, planning on copying yourself lots as an
   upload, or planning on reproducing via your memes rather than your genes,
   then you may want to save more than folks following their evolution-given
   time preferences.

B) 15-25 year olds less likely to be receptive to extropian discussions
   of the future.

C) Uploads and AIs will likely reproduce asexually at first.  If
   evolution dictates their values, they will likely discount the future at
   their population growth rate, which is likely the economy's growth rate.

D) Time preferences of non-evolving ordinary humans will likely not be very 
   responsive to huge increases in the economic growth rate.  If wealth 
   doubles every year, humans will invest and save like the dickens.  

