From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Tue, 28 Jun 94 00:14:11 EDT
Subject: [#94-6-174] Scenarios: What happens when nanotechnology appears? 

Ray and Robin Hanson make several valuable points and I thank them for their
constructive responses.  I believe we agree in many areas; however, we still
have some central disagreements.

This note discusses the differences between the way one should think
about pre-nanotech war and nanotech war. A separate note will discuss
prenanotech war.

When examining the possibility and the consequences of a nanotech war I
believe that several important new issues are introduced.  I agree with
Robin that it is useful to ``think more about plain old fashion war''.
However, a nanotech war will be characterized by new and radical forms of
uncertainty which are only comparatively weakly present in current wars.

Consider the military scenario given in the following excerpt from Unbounding
the Future pg 32

Disputes over technology development and trade had soured relationships
between Singapore and the Japan-United States alliance.  Diplomatic
inquiries regarding peculiar seismic and sonar readings in the South China
Sea had just begun when they suddenly became irrelevant: an estimated one
billion tons of unfamiliar, highly automated military hardware appeared in
coastal waters around the world.

End of excerpt

What is different about nanotech war versus current war?  Here are some
thoughts:

1) When nanotech is developed the design space for weapons will open up
dramatically.  Warring factions must quickly search this design space to
heuristically select which weapons they consider to be the best offensive and
defensive weapons.

2) The design space will be changing quickly and unpredictably as new
advancements are made in science and technology and as new capabilities are
realized.

3) The time scale for the development and deployment of weapons will be
dramatically compressed. If development is assisted by automated computerized
systems using advanced AI then humans may not even fully understand the
weapons that are deployed. Even if you do not think AI will be useful the
time scale will still shrink.

4) Warring factions may suffer from radical uncertainty about the nature of
opposition weaponry. They may be uncertain about what weapons will be
deployed against them in the future and more frighteningly they may even be
uncertain about the nature of weapons that are currently deployed against
them.

5) In the current pre-nanotech environment warring parties can relatively
accurately gauge the power of adversaries and potential adversaries.  For
example, currently, politicians are worrying about whether North Korea may
have a handful of nuclear weapons.  They also worry about whether North
Korea will in the future develop a handful more and possibly sell them to
other nations.  Politicians do not worry about whether North Korea has ``a
billion tons of unfamiliar, highly automated military hardware'' hidden in
the oceans.  They also do not worry about whether North Korea will develop a
billion tons of military hardware in a few months.

5) The radical uncertainties of this new kind of warfare may result in a
strong temptation to attempt to incapacitate an enemy by launching a
preemptive strike. A preemptive strike may be based on a weapon (or set of
weapons) which are believed to be stealthy (with respect to the current
detection technology of the adversary). Alternatively, the preemptive strike
may be based on a weapon (or set of weapons) which are believed capable of
incapacitating the enemy in a way which precludes any significant response.
Of course these beliefs can be tragically wrong.

6) As nanotech develops the balance between offensive and defensive war
technology may swing wildly.  It is conceivable that large numbers of
relatively small groups may obtain offensive technology which can not be
effectively defended against and which can destroy substantial fractions of
humans and human-level entities.  (Even without nanotech nuclear
proliferation suggests this possibility.  But nuclear technology appears
easier to contain than nanotech.)  From among these many groups, one or more
may follow military strategies which we would deem ``insane'', ``murderous''
or ``suicidal''.  These groups may be motivated by religious or
philosophical doctrines which consider the destruction mankind an acceptable
or desirable possibility. Ray refers to a ``nutcase'' releasing a
``nanostrike''.

Consider how current war doctrines may be modified by a nanotech war:

Ray says about the two nation scenario I gave:
>  In the two nation scenario, each with the capability of destroying
>the other, the MAD equilibrium should prevent the scenario from being
>carried out.

Mutually assured destruction (MAD) was a useful doctrine when the two
adversaries could accurately gauge the military power of one another; when
technological development was relatively slow; and when both sides were
``sane''.  I would contend that the points enumerated above make MAD
dangerously unstable.  I think MAD is unstable even when both sides are not
``hell-bent on wiping the other off the map''.  All it takes is a little
fear and/or mistrust.  As indicated in point five a preemptive attack would
be tempting for both sides.  The probability of a  disastrous miscalculation
is heightened by the uncertainty inherent in a nanotech war environment.  If
attack(s) are launched then one side may be victorious; a new stalemate may
occur; or dual annihilation may result.  A new stalemate is more likely than
dual annihilation if the attacks can be shut-down, recalled or revoked. Not
all attacks allow these possibilities.

I believe a nanotech war environment also differs from a prenanotech war
environment by its probabilistic distribution of likely results.  A
prenanotech war environment is more likely to lead to a ``cold'' war or a
``stalemated'' war then a nanotech war environment.  Point five supports
this.

If a war actually occurs then the probability of total annihilation (of
humans and/or life) is larger in a nanotech war then prenanotech.  Points
five and six support this.  However, I do not think annihilation is
inevitable.

If a nanotech war occurs I think there is a substantial chance that a single
``unified massively distributed being'' will be the victor.  I have not
defined what such a being is. It may sound flaky to the readers of this note
but I think it is an interesting concept. I will post about it some time and
you can try to critique or tear apart the concept if you are unconvinced.


What kinds of weapons systems will be deployed in a nanotech war?

Ray makes several good points about simplistic gray goo scenarios.

The name gray goo probably mislead Ray about the kinds of weapon systems I
envision in a nanowar. I mentioned using explosions, heat, projectiles etc
and using functional units of varying size.  The Drexler scenario at the
beginning of this message is relevant.

Here is one vision. Consider massive assemblages of tightly coordinated
ships, planes, tanks, robots etc. controlled by flexible, fault-tolerant,
distributed AI.  The names of the components are somewhat misleading because
they will be enormous improvements over what we currently call ships,
planes, tanks and robots.  Also, components much larger and much smaller
than the items listed will be part of the team. This includes bioagents to
attack human biological vulnerabilities.

It will not be necessary for all components to be capable of replication.
Typically, replicators will build the assembly lines which will yield the
military hardware. 

The replicators will not need to replicate in the Antarctica. Some of the
military components should be specially designed to work in the Antarctica;
other components should work in the Sahara etc.  The Drexler scenario has
the replicators apparently working in the South China Sea. Also the
replicators will not blindly replicate. Replication will be carefully
limited based on extensive dynamically gathered knowledge on the available
resources.  I think replicators might work well in large numbers of big oil
fields.  There is plenty of carbon for diamondoid structures and energy is
available.  I am too tired to look at the size of proven oil reserves to see
how many tons of military hardware you will get from this method.
The military hardware will fan out from one or more sites of creation
to confront the ``enemies''.

What about the design problem? Will it be too hard to design?  It will be
very hard. An error could cause system failure or worse self-annihilation.
Desire for self preservation in a very dangerous world will still push
forward the design of weapons systems like this I believe.  Automated
engineering and AI could help.  


What if humans can get off of earth? I think this will help greatly.
Unfortunately, I suspect in the early stages the humans off Earth will be in
vulnerable cocoons: spaceships, O'Neill type colonies or colonies on planet
surfaces.  An enemy will try to design military hardware to track down and
incapacitate humans in space as well as on Earth. Some list members are very
knowledgable about space colonization etc and may have opinions about this.

Ray asks:
>   If replicators are so good at outcompetiting macroscopic life, why
>don't bacteria rule the earth? Must be something inefficient about
>small simple lifeforms.


By weight I think that insects are a larger fraction of the biosphere than
humans. In that limited sense insects outcompete humans. I also think that
single celled lifeforms are an even bigger fraction of the biosphere but I
am not sure so I will not claim it.

Anyway the weapons systems I am discussing are best viewed as very large
unified distributed beings. They will not be dumb like bacteria or insects.
In aggregate they will be very smart. They will have super-human intelligence
if AI succeeds.

I am leaning toward agreeing with Drexler that we should have international
cooperation and tight multi-lateral international governmental controls on
the development of nanotechnology.; even though I see many problems with
a powerful `state'.


